<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<?import javafx.geometry.Insets?>
<?import javafx.scene.control.Button?>
<?import javafx.scene.control.Label?>
<?import javafx.scene.control.ProgressBar?>
<?import javafx.scene.control.Tooltip?>
<?import javafx.scene.layout.HBox?>
<?import javafx.scene.layout.Region?>
<?import javafx.scene.layout.StackPane?>
<?import javafx.scene.layout.VBox?>
<?import javafx.scene.shape.SVGPath?>

<!--
    ============================================================================
    FLOATING TIMER  (v2.0)
    ============================================================================
    A minimal always-on-top widget for running a focus session while the main
    dashboard is hidden. Deliberately tiny: it is meant to sit in a corner of
    the screen and stay out of the way, so it carries a clock, a progress bar,
    the target skill, and three buttons. Nothing else.

    ============================================================================
    WHY THIS FILE HAS NO TIMER IN IT
    ============================================================================
    There is no AnimationTimer, no Timeline and no countdown state anywhere in
    this view, and that is the single most important property of the design.

    The obvious implementation gives the widget its own timer that ticks down a
    local counter. Two consequences follow immediately:

      1. DRIFT. Two independent tickers never agree. The dashboard would read
         24:59 while the widget read 24:58, and a user who trusted one of them
         would be misled. Worse, whichever tick is scheduled later keeps
         accumulating small rounding differences over a 25-minute session.
      2. A HANDOFF GAP. Popping out or docking would have to transfer a counter
         between two owners. That transfer is exactly where seconds go missing,
         and it is the failure the v2.0 requirement ("the timer must not reset
         if the application is closed") is really about, just made smaller.

    So the widget is a PURE OBSERVER. DashboardController owns the single
    AnimationTimer and the single absolute deadline, and each tick it computes
    the remaining time ONCE and pushes that single value to every view. Two
    views therefore cannot disagree, and the pop-out handoff involves no time
    arithmetic at all - there is no counter to copy, only a label to re-parent.

    ============================================================================
    WHY THE ROOT IS A TRANSPARENT STACKPane
    ============================================================================
    The stage is StageStyle.TRANSPARENT, which requires BOTH:
      - the scene fill set to null (done in FloatingTimerWindow), and
      - the root node's own background transparent.
    The outer StackPane is transparent and only the inner card carries a
    background, so the user sees a rounded card floating over their desktop with
    no rectangular window border around it.

    The 14px padding on the StackPane is deliberate dead space: it gives the
    drop shadow room to render without being clipped by the stage bounds, which
    is what produces the hard dark edge that makes a transparent JavaFX stage
    look cheap.

    pickOnBounds="true" ON THE ROOT, and it is not cosmetic.

    With pickOnBounds="false" the root only claims a mouse press where it has
    painted something. The padding ring is transparent by definition, so that
    whole border of the window was not the widget's - a press that landed in it
    fell through to the window behind. During a drag that produced the worst
    possible symptom: the widget would follow the cursor across the card, then
    die the moment the cursor reached the edge, and come back only after the
    user pressed again. Windows' DWM does per-pixel hit testing on a layered
    transparent window, so a fully transparent pixel really does belong to
    whatever is underneath.

    pickOnBounds makes the root claim its whole rectangular bounds regardless of
    paint, which is exactly what a drag target needs. It does NOT make the root
    visible - paint is a separate question - so the widget still looks like a
    floating card with a soft shadow rather than a rectangle.
    ============================================================================
-->
<StackPane xmlns="http://javafx.com/javafx" xmlns:fx="http://javafx.com/fxml"
           fx:controller="com.unitracker.controller.FloatingTimerController"
           styleClass="float-timer-root" pickOnBounds="true">

    <!-- The card. fx:id'd so the controller can drag the window by it, since a
         transparent stage has no title bar to drag.

         NOTE THE COMMA. FXML's styleClass attribute is COMMA-separated, not
         space-separated. This is the one genuinely easy thing to get wrong
         here, because a space-separated list loads with no error at all and
         produces a single style class literally named
         "glass-panel float-timer-card" - which then matches no CSS rule. The
         widget appears completely unstyled and still runs, so the only way to
         catch it is an assertion like devcheck/FxmlCheck's, which checks the
         card actually carries the class. -->
    <VBox fx:id="card" styleClass="glass-panel, float-timer-card" spacing="8"
          pickOnBounds="true" mouseTransparent="false">

        <!-- Drag handle row: skill name on the left, dock button on the right.
             The whole card is draggable, but an explicit affordance here tells
             the user that the thing is movable. -->
        <HBox alignment="CENTER_LEFT" spacing="8">
            <!-- A long skill name must not stretch the card past its fixed
                 stage width, so the label is capped and CLIPS.
                 JavaFX 25's Labeled has no ellipsis or truncation property at
                 all (both "ellipsis" and "textTruncationPolicy" throw
                 PropertyNotFoundException), so a tooltip carries the full name
                 instead - see setToolTip in FloatingTimerController. -->
            <Label fx:id="skillLabel" text="" styleClass="float-timer-skill"
                   maxWidth="210"/>
            <Region HBox.hgrow="ALWAYS"/>
            <!-- v2.0: SVGPath, NOT a text glyph.

                 This button used to carry text="&#x23CB;" (U+23CB RETURN
                 SYMBOL) and rendered as a solid white box on any machine whose
                 fonts lack that codepoint. The return/enter arrows live in
                 obscure symbol-plane fonts that many Windows images simply do
                 not ship, and a missing glyph renders as a filled box - so the
                 button read as an unstyled white rectangle.

                 An SVGPath is drawn by JavaFX itself and is therefore identical
                 on every machine, with no font dependency at all.

                 It is wrapped in a fixed-size StackPane because a Button whose
                 only content is a graphic has no intrinsic width of its own;
                 without the wrapper the button collapses to nothing.

                 Coordinates are in a 0..16 box, so the optical size matches
                 what a text glyph would have had.

                 THREE SPELLING TRAPS IN THE ATTRIBUTES BELOW, all of
                 which fail at LOAD time rather than at compile time:

                 1. fill="transparent", NOT fill="none". "none" is correct
                    CSS, but FXML coerces a Paint through Paint.valueOf ->
                    Color.web(), which knows named colours and "transparent"
                    and nothing else. "none" throws "Invalid color
                    specification". JavaFX's Shape default fill is black, so
                    the transparent has to be stated.
                 2. strokeWidth / strokeLineCap / strokeLineJoin, NOT
                    stroke-width / stroke-line-cap / stroke-linejoin. Those
                    hyphenated forms are the -fx- CSS property names, and
                    this is a BEAN property on Shape, not a CSS rule. FXML
                    does not translate between the two vocabularies.
                 3. #E7ECF3 rather than a lookup colour name, so the icon
                    cannot change under a theme swap. -->
            <Button fx:id="dockButton" styleClass="icon-button, float-timer-dock">
                <graphic>
                    <StackPane prefWidth="16" prefHeight="16">
                        <SVGPath content="M 13 8 L 3 8 M 9 2 L 3 8 L 9 14"
                                 fill="transparent" stroke="#E7ECF3" strokeWidth="1.7"
                                 strokeLineCap="round" strokeLineJoin="round"/>
                    </StackPane>
                </graphic>
                <tooltip><Tooltip text="Put the timer back in the dashboard"/></tooltip>
            </Button>
        </HBox>

        <!-- The clock. minWidth="0" is load-bearing: without it the label pins
             at its full text width and, on a long hh:mm:ss, forces the card
             wider than the stage and clips the buttons. -->
        <Label fx:id="clockLabel" text="25:00" styleClass="float-timer-clock"
               minWidth="0" maxWidth="260" alignment="CENTER"/>

        <ProgressBar fx:id="progressBar" progress="0" maxWidth="Infinity"
                     styleClass="pomodoro-bar"/>

        <!-- Controls. minWidth="0" on the spacer rather than hgrow on the
             clock for the same reason the sidebar panel does it that way: an
             hgrow-ing clock would claim space it does not need and shove the
             buttons off the card at small sizes. -->
        <HBox alignment="CENTER" spacing="8">
            <Button fx:id="playPauseButton" text="Pause" styleClass="accent-button, compact-button"/>
            <!-- Same reasoning as the dock button: a reset glyph in a symbol
                 font is a missing-glyph box on most machines. -->
            <Button fx:id="resetButton" styleClass="icon-button">
                <graphic>
                    <StackPane prefWidth="16" prefHeight="16">
                        <SVGPath content="M 13.5 5.5 A 5.5 5.5 0 1 0 14 9"
                                 fill="transparent" stroke="#E7ECF3" strokeWidth="1.7"
                                 strokeLineCap="round"/>
                        <SVGPath content="M 13.5 2.2 L 13.5 5.6 L 10.1 5.6"
                                 fill="transparent" stroke="#E7ECF3" strokeWidth="1.7"
                                 strokeLineCap="round" strokeLineJoin="round"/>
                    </StackPane>
                </graphic>
                <tooltip><Tooltip text="Discard this session and start fresh"/></tooltip>
            </Button>
        </HBox>

        <Label fx:id="stateLabel" text="" styleClass="float-timer-state"/>
    </VBox>
</StackPane>
